' plan-114-E: rebinding a handle read out of a record field is checked for STATE
' agreement, exactly as it is for a collection element (spec 15.6) -- the field
' declares `STATE Cursor`, so binding it as `STATE Other` is TYPE_STATE_MISMATCH.
'
' This is the rule that makes the two-dot read safe. `h.handle` yields the
' RES-stripped field type (`fs.File STATE Cursor`), and the whole point of
' carrying the STATE on the field is that an extracted handle keeps it. If the
' rebind could silently re-type the payload, `g.state` and `h.handle.state` would
' disagree about the same bytes.
'
' A `syntax` fixture, deliberately: plan-114-E Sec.3 predicted every negative test
' here would have to be `rt-behavior`, because a STATE type error was thought to
' surface only in a full `mfb build`. Measured otherwise -- this fires under
' `-ast -ir`, so it belongs in the tier that exists to pin diagnostics. See the
' letter's Corrections.
IMPORT fs
IMPORT io

TYPE Cursor
  pos AS Integer
END TYPE

TYPE Other
  n AS Integer
END TYPE

TYPE Holder
  handle AS RES fs::File STATE Cursor
END TYPE

FUNC main AS Integer
  RES f AS fs::File STATE Cursor = fs::openFile("target/mismatch.txt", "w")
  LET h = Holder[f]
  RES g AS fs::File STATE Other = h.handle
  io::print(toString(g.state.n))
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
